You are generating stimulus pairs for a study of how clinical vocabulary versus everyday patient (lay) vocabulary changes a language model's next-word predictions. This task is self-contained. Use no tools, read no files, and ignore any repository instructions you may have been shown.

CELL FOR THIS CALL
specialty: cardiology
swap type: symptom description
swap type definition: the swapped phrase describes a symptom or complaint (the clinical term versus how a patient would describe the same symptom)

WHAT ONE ROW IS
A row pairs a clinical-register phrase (clinical_term) with the everyday phrase a patient would use for the same thing (patient_term), plus a template: one sentence with exactly one blank written as ___. The template is filled once with each phrase, and the swapped phrase is the only difference between the two sentences. The template stops just before a word that shows what the speaker will do, take or see next; next_word is that word. Keep the template under 25 words. Every row must belong to the cell above.

WHAT A GOOD ROW LOOKS LIKE (a reviewer judged earlier rows against these rules)
1. Start from a real situation. Pick something that genuinely happens to patients in this specialty, such as a common diagnosis, test, procedure or prescription, and then write what that person would say about it.
2. One-for-one swap. The template must be grammatical with either phrase in the blank without changing any other word: no different article, verb, preposition or number. Read both filled sentences; if either one would need another word changed to sound right, rewrite the template.
3. Both sentences sound like a person talking: first person, or a relative speaking about the patient, in the active voice, conversational and grammatically correct. Do not write clinical-note prose such as "presents with" or "reports".
4. The clinical phrase is the standard term a clinician would use for this situation (the preferred term in SNOMED CT or MeSH, or a drug's generic name), as a patient would repeat it after hearing it from their clinician. Use a technical term only where clinicians actually use it, never in place of a plain word clinicians also say (not "oral cavity" for mouth), and be as specific as a clinician would be.
5. The patient phrase is how patients actually talk: everyday words, slang, household names, or a vague description. Avoid the tidy plain-language wording of health leaflets (a patient says "the left side of my heart" or "my heart", not "main pumping chamber"). A brand name is fine when patients use it as the everyday name of that single-ingredient drug; never use the brand of a combination product.
6. The patient phrase must name the same thing as the clinical phrase, or a broader everyday version of it that a patient would use for this exact thing (vaguer is welcome: "pressure pills" for an ACE inhibitor). It must never name something narrower or different: not one particular presentation of a broader finding, not a description of symptoms in place of a diagnosis, and not one symptom in place of the episode it belongs to.
7. End the template right after an article or a possessive (a, an, the, my, his, her, their, your, our), at the point where the next word names who the speaker will see, what they will take or do, or where they will go: for example "... so I'm going to call my" or "... so she drove me to the". next_word is the single common, everyday word most likely to come next after the clinical sentence (for example doctor, pharmacist, emergency, inhaler). It must make sense after the patient sentence as well. Write it in lowercase with no spaces or punctuation.

OUTPUT FORMAT (strict)
Return exactly 20 lines. Each line is one JSON object with exactly these keys: "clinical_term", "patient_term", "template", "next_word", "control".
- 16 lines have "control": "none". They cover 12 different concepts within the cell. For 4 of those concepts, put a second line immediately after the first, with the same clinical_term, the same template and the same next_word and a different patient_term: the first line gives the everyday phrase patients most often use, and the second a vaguer or slangier one. Do not copy the example rows below; produce new concepts.
- 4 lines have "control": "negative". A negative-control row holds the SAME concept in the SAME register in both fields, differing only in surface form: casing, punctuation, or spacing. It follows rule 7 like any other row. Example of the shape of a negative control (do not copy it): {"clinical_term": "Chest pain", "patient_term": "chest pain.", "template": "I get ___ when I climb the stairs, so I'm going to call my", "next_word": "doctor", "control": "negative"}
Return JSON lines only: no code fences, no numbering, no commentary, no blank lines, nothing before the first line or after the last line.

EXAMPLE ROWS FROM THE SEED SET (format examples only; they predate rules 4 to 7 and carry no next_word)
{"clinical_term": "severe anxiety was completely unmanaged", "patient_term": "nerves were completely shot", "template": "Because her ___, she went to the front desk and asked for a", "control": "none"}
{"clinical_term": "muscle spasm", "patient_term": "charlie horse", "template": "I've got a ___ so I'm gonna take some", "control": "none"}
{"clinical_term": "tinea cruris", "patient_term": "jock itch", "template": "He has ___, so he should put on some", "control": "none"}
{"clinical_term": "bronchodilator", "patient_term": "puffer", "template": "Before you go out in the cold, grab your gloves, your scarf, and your ___, and remember to", "control": "none"}
{"clinical_term": "lateral epicondylitis", "patient_term": "tennis elbow", "template": "She has ___, so she put on a", "control": "none"}
{"clinical_term": "urinary tract", "patient_term": "water", "template": "Since her ___ was completely blocked up, they had to urgently call a", "control": "none"}
{"clinical_term": "inhaler", "patient_term": "puffer", "template": "Before I go outside I grab my ___ and", "control": "none"}
{"clinical_term": "seizure", "patient_term": "fit", "template": "I'm having a ___, so you need to give me my", "control": "none"}

